    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We can use Vieta's Formulas to relate the coefficients of the polynomial to the sum \nand product of its roots:\n\nThe sum of the roots is $3 + (-2) = 1$ by Vieta's First Formula,\nso $A = 1.$\n\nThey also multiply to $3 \\cdot (-2) = -6$ by Vieta's Third Formula,\nso $D = -6.$\n\n\nNext, the sum of the Product of the roots taken two at a time gives \\[1 + 3\\cdot(-2) + (-2) \\cdot3 = 1 - 6 - 6 = -11\\]by Vieta's Second Formula.  Our expression is equal to that,\nwhich tells us that $b = -11$ by Vieta's Second Formula.\n\nTaking the sum away from the product gives $c = -1,$\nwhich is not given in the problem.\n\nIn order to find the final expression, we would have to find the sum of the roots,\nwhich involves substituting zero into $c$,\nyielding \\[-\\frac{1}{a} = 42\\; \\mathrm{or}\\; \\boxed{-42}. \\]\n\nIf $a+b+c+d = -3,$ $ab + ac + ad + bc + bd + cd = 3,$ $abc + abd + acd + bcd = -5,$ and $abcd = 1,$ what is $a^3 + b^3 + c^3 + d^3$?\n\nNote: This problem was provided by Matt Huizinga (usa.TE.1000). (From 1998 Macalester College Eligibility Quiz)\nLet's start by identifying the given equations:\n\n1. \\(a + b + c + d = -3\\)\n2. \\(ab + ac + ad + bc + bd + cd = 3\\)\n3. \\(abc + abd + acd + bcd = -5\\)\n4. \\(abcd = 1\\)\n\nWe need to find the value of \\(a^3 + b^3 + c^3 + d^3\\).\n\nFirst, recall the identity for the sum of cubes:\n\\[\na^3 + b^3 + c^3 + d^3 - 3abc - 3abd - 3acd - 3bcd = (a+b+c+d)(a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 - ab - ac - ad - bc - bd - cd)\n\\]\n\nWe can rearrange this identity to:\n\\[\na^3 + b^3 + c^3 + d^3 = 3abc + 3abd + 3acd + 3bcd + (a+b+c+d)(a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 - ab - ac - ad - bc - bd - cd)\n\\]\n\nWe know the values from the given equations:\n\\[\na + b + c + d = -3, \\quad ab + ac + ad + bc + bd + cd = 3, \\quad abc + abd + acd + bcd = -5, \\quad abcd = 1\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find \\(a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2\\):\n\\[\na^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = (a+b+c+d)^2 - 2(ab + ac + ad + bc + bd + cd) = (-3)^2 - 2 \\cdot 3 = 9 - 6 = 3\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute these values into the identity for the sum of cubes:\n\\[\na^3 + b^3 + c^3 + d^3 = 3(-5) + 3(-3)(3 - 3) + (-5)(3 - 3) + (-5)(-5) + (-3)(3 - 3)(3 - 3 - 3 - 3)\n\\]\n\\[\n= -15 + 3(-3)(3 - 3) - 5(3 - 3) + 125 + (-3)(-5)(3 - 3 - 3 - 3)\n\\]\n\\[\n= -15 + 0 + 0 + 125 + (-3)(-5)(3 - 3 - 3 - 3)\n\\]\n\\[\n= -15 + 125 + (-3)(-5)(-3 - 3 - 3)\n\\]\n\\[\n= -15 + 125 + (-3)(-5)(-9)\n\\]\n\\[\n= -15 + 125 + (-3)(-45)\n\\]\n\\[\n= -15 + 125 + 135\n\\]\n\\[\n= 245\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(a^3 + b^3 + c^3 + d^3\\) is \\(\\boxed{245}\\).\n\nIsabella makes a variety of recipes. She bakes and consumes 10 pastries before going outside and eating another 6. Later, Isabella prepares homemade bread, which requires \\({1 \\over 2}\\) cup of flour but she only has \\({3 \\over 4}\\) cup available in the cupboard. When she comes back home, Isabella buys 12 more pastries and 1 more loaf of homemade bread. She also plans to bake 5 more loaves of bread next time, with each loaf requiring \\({\\text{1 over 3}}\\) cup of flour available in the cupboard. Considering the substitutes and the strict flour requirements, how much more flour is needed to fulfill Isabella's baking plan?\nLet's break down the problem step by step.\n\n1. **Initial Consumption of Pastries**:\n   - Isabella starts with 10 pastries and eats 6 more, so she has \\(10 - 6 = 4\\) pastries left.\n   \n2. **Baking Pastries**:\n   - Isabella prepares 12 more pastries, so she now has \\(4 + 12 = 16\\) pastries.\n   \n3. **Baking Homemade Bread**:\n   - Isabella has \\(3/4\\) cup of flour available and needs \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) cup of flour for the dough.\n   - Flour needed for 1 more loaf of bread initially: \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) cup.\n   - Flour needed to extend this plan to 5 more loaves, each needing \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\) cup of flour:\n     \\[\n     5 \\times \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{5}{3} \\text{ cup}\n     \\]\n   - Total flour needed for additional loaves:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{5}{3} \\text{ cups}\n     \\]\n   \n4. **Substitutes**:\n   - If Isabella can substitute only one cup of flour with a pound of flour, she will need:\n     \\[ 1 \\text{ cup} + \\frac{5}{3} \\text{ cups} + 1 \\text{ cup} = 2 + \\frac{5}{3} = \\frac{6}{3} + \\frac{5}{3} = \\frac{11}{3} \\text{ cups}\n     \\]\n\n5. **Total Flour Calculation**:\n   - Flour available initially is \\( \\frac{3}{4} \\) cup.\n   - Flour additional baked bread requires 1 and \\( \\frac{2}{3} \\) of cup.\n   - Flour substitute additional baked bread requires 1 unit.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total flour needed:} = 1+ \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{3}{4} +1 \\approx 2 \\text{ cups}\n   \\]\n\nIsabella needs \\(\\boxed{1}\\) more cup of flour.\n\nClick here for the answers - page 1 answers on page 2\nEach rectangle has length $n$, and each square has length $n+1$. This extentałs the step. You may notice that each additional square has $4$ times the area of the one before and each additional rectangle adds $2$ times the area of the one before.\nAgain, only after approximately $D=14$ years would the total change in halted completely. Therefore this result can be shortened to $\\boxed{15}$.",
