      "text": "Given that the quadratic equation \\[ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\] has roots \\(3\\) and \\(-2\\), we can express it as:\n\\[a(x - 3)(x + 2) = 0.\\]\nExpanding the product, we get:\n\\[a(x^2 - x - 6) = ax^2 - ax - 6a.\\]\nThis matches the general form \\(ax^2 + bx + c\\), where:\n\\[b = -a\\quad \\text{and} \\quad c = -6a.\\]\nWe can now find \\(\\frac{b+c}{a}\\) by substituting \\(b\\) and \\(c\\):\n\\[\\frac{b+c}{a} = \\frac{-a + (-6a)}{a} = \\frac{-7a}{a} = -7.\\]\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-7}\n\\]",
